# kanso play bugs/err_in_list_literal.kso
#
# SHOULD: reach the endpoint with "too big", the way the same err does when
# it is interpolated, passed to a function or handed to an operator. Chapter
# 04: "an err flows through functions, not into them".
#
# DOES: prints `1 [err "too big"]` on both engines. A list literal holds the
# err as an element, `length` counts it, and the program exits 0. In
# xsv/selection.kso a selector that failed to resolve came back as a
# one-element list holding the failure, and the selection "succeeded".
fn checked n
  return err "too big" if n > 5
  n

fn boxed n
  one = checked n
  [one]

print "{length (boxed 9)} {boxed 9}"
